<article class="ltx_document">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: 
<br class="ltx_break">What to Retrieve and How to Use It?</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">David S. Hippocampus
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_funding ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Use footnote for providing further information
about author (webpage, alternative address)—<em id="id1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> for acknowledging
funding agencies.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Science
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Cranberry-Lemon University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Pittsburgh, PA 15213
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">hippo@cs.cranberry-lemon.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="866.89" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 866.89" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,866.89) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F3F5;" fill="#F2F3F5" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 11.07 L 0 855.82 C 0 861.93 4.96 866.89 11.07 866.89 L 538.93 866.89 C 545.04 866.89 550 861.93 550 855.82 L 550 11.07 C 550 4.96 545.04 0 538.93 0 L 11.07 0 C 4.96 0 0 4.96 0 11.07 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F3F5;" fill="#F2F3F5" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 11.07 L 0 855.82 C 0 861.93 4.96 866.89 11.07 866.89 L 538.93 866.89 C 545.04 866.89 550 861.93 550 855.82 L 550 11.07 C 550 4.96 545.04 0 538.93 0 L 11.07 0 C 4.96 0 0 4.96 0 11.07 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.75 18.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:37.47em;--ltx-fo-height:60.18em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="835.4" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 832.71)" width="518.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:37.47em;">
<span id="p1.pic1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<span id="p1.pic1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: 
<br class="ltx_break">What to Retrieve and How to Use It?</span></span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Yuho Lee<sup id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>, Jisu Shin<sup id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>, Nicole Hee-Yeon Kim<sup id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>, Jihwan Bang<sup id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>, Juntae Lee<sup id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.5" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>, Kyuwoong Hwang<sup id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.6" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>, Fatih Porikli<sup id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.7" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup> Hwanjun Song<sup id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.8" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.2.1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup></span></span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><sup id="p1.pic1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup><span id="p1.pic1.p3.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">KAIST, <sup id="p1.pic1.p3.3.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>Qualcomm AI Research<sup id="p1.pic1.p3.3.2.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p3.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup>, Qualcomm Korea YH, Seoul, Republic of Korea</span></span>
</span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="p1.pic1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Retrieval-augmented generation is moving beyond text into long, egocentric video, where systems must select query-relevant chunks across multiple modalities and temporal granularities. Yet progress in </span><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">VideoRAG</span><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is limited by two gaps: existing benchmarks allow queries to be answered without the video, obscuring retrieval errors, and prior methods apply a single modality-granularity configuration per query, ignoring chunk-level variability. We address both by introducing </span><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">V-RAGBench</span><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, a benchmark of </span><math id="p1.pic1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">query, evidence chunk, answer</span><math id="p1.pic1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rangle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> triplets that enables faithful, decoupled evaluation of retrieval and generation, and </span><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">CARVE</span><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, a simple method that runs parallel retrievers across configurations and employs chunk-adaptive reranking to identify the winning configuration for each chunk. Each chunk then enters the generator under its winning configuration selected during retrieval, yielding an interleaved evidence form where the chunk-level decision propagates across both stages. </span><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">CARVE</span><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> outperforms eight recent </span><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">VideoRAG</span><span id="p1.pic1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> baselines, with the chunks supplied to the generator interleaving multiple configurations rather than sharing a single one, a behavior unattainable by query-level methods.</span></span>
</span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<span id="p1.pic1.p4.1" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:243.6pt;">
<span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Date:</span><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> June 11, 2026</span></span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Correspondence:</span><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Hwanjun Song
(</span><a href="mailto:songhwanjun@kaist.ac.kr" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0066CC;">songhwanjun@kaist.ac.kr</a><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">First Authors (equal contribution):</span></span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Yuho Lee (</span><a href="mailto:yuholee@kaist.ac.kr" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0066CC;">yuholee@kaist.ac.kr</a><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.4.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Jisu Shin (</span><a href="mailto:jisu3389@kaist.ac.kr" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0066CC;">jisu3389@kaist.ac.kr</a><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.5.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Nicole Hee-Yeon Kim (</span><a href="mailto:nicole2kim@gmail.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0066CC;">nicole2kim@gmail.com</a><span id="p1.pic1.p4.1.6.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></span>
</span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p4.2" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:86.2pt;"><img src="2606.13141v1/figures/dislab-icon-Photoroom.png" id="p1.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_align_right ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:119/45;" width="119" height="45" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"><img src="2606.13141v1/figures/qc_AIResearch_RGB-removebg-preview.png" id="p1.pic1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_align_right ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:119/60;" width="119" height="60" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
</span>
</span>
<span id="p1.pic1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="p1.pic1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><sup id="p1.pic1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="p1.pic1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">†</span></sup><span id="p1.pic1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Qualcomm AI Research is an initiative of Qualcomm Technologies, Inc.</span></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) grounds large language models (LLMs) in external knowledge, improving factual reliability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>]</cite>. Its frontier is now moving beyond static text corpora toward video-centric settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>, where the primary source of knowledge is no longer curated text but <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">long-form video</em> in the <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">egocentric</em> setting, as wearable devices turn daily life into long first-person videos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> and agentic systems operate directly on accumulated personal video data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>. We refer to this setting, RAG over long videos, as <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>, where the system needs to retrieve query-relevant evidence from a long video. This shift does not merely extend retrieval but fundamentally increases its retrieval and generation complexity. Given a target query, the system must retrieve query-relevant video chunks by matching across <em id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multimodal representations</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>, such as visual features and higher-level textual abstractions, as well as across multiple <em id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">temporal granularities</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, from frame-level details to clip-level segments. The problem thus moves beyond what to retrieve to deciding "which modality and which granularity to retrieve from?" and "how to exploit them jointly across retrieval and generation?"</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite this growing interest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, current benchmark datasets have not kept pace. While hour-scale source videos are available through Ego4D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and EgoLife <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>, the queries paired with them are scarce and were not designed for <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>. In particular, recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> shows that over half of widely used video QA samples can be answered without the video at all, relying on linguistic priors, world knowledge, or static cues. Such queries are fatal for <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>, since a system can reach correct answers even when retrieval surfaces irrelevant chunks, so <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">high generation accuracy does not imply successful retrieval</em>, and final accuracy fails as a proxy for the retrieval stage. Under such evaluation, the right way to use modality and temporal granularity across retrieval and generation cannot be identified, as each design choice is invisible behind the final accuracy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13141v1/method_main.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/98;" width="476" height="98" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The overview of <span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span>: Stage 1 builds a candidate pool via chunk-wise parallel retrieval, while Stage 2 performs chunk-adaptive reranking. The final top-<math id="S1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> evidence with their winning configuration is passed to the generator in a modality-interleaved form.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The same gap appears on the methodological side. Recent work proposes techniques for <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>, such as guided query refinement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>, modality fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>, and query decomposition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, while others delegate retrieval to iterative agentic loops <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite> that act more as test-time scaling than as principled retrieval. Although retrieval is the core component on which all these frameworks ultimately rest, its validation is left at a shallow level, reduced to downstream QA accuracy on the full pipeline. This is in sharp contrast to text-centric RAG, where retrieval is treated as a first-class object of study and audited through stage-wise metrics on query-evidence pairs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We address this gap by introducing <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span>, a dedicated benchmark designed to make retrieval and generation in <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> faithfully measurable both in isolation and in their interaction. <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span> consists of <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle</annotation></semantics></math>query, evidence chunk, answer<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> triplets drawn from long egocentric videos in Ego4D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and EgoLife <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>, totaling <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{,}100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{,}100</annotation></semantics></math> high-quality triplets across <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="216" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>216</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">216</annotation></semantics></math> videos spanning <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> hours. Targeting the <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> setting, <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span> jointly enforces three properties that no existing dataset satisfies together: each query is (i) <em id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tied to non-recurring evidence</em>, so that the underlying event does not repeat elsewhere in the video as a near-duplicate; (ii) <em id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">visually-grounded</em>, so that the answer is not implied by the query itself or recoverable from parametric knowledge alone; and (iii) <em id="S1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evidence-localized</em>, so that the answer cannot be reconstructed from generic visual cues that incidentally recur across non-evidence chunks. Property (i) is enforced via temporal segmentation and clustering, while (ii) and (iii) are enforced by dedicated post-hoc filters. Together, these properties make generation causally dependent on retrieval, enabling a stage-wise view of <span id="S1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span> in hand, we shift the main question of <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> from improving QA accuracy to which representation of a video chunk best supports each stage of the pipeline, namely retrieval and generation. Each chunk admits four candidate configurations<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          Other retrieval signals, such as video knowledge graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> or metadata (<em id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, explicit temporal clue) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, can aid retrieval but are not consistently available. We focus on content-based representations, which are universally accessible.</span></span></span>, formed by crossing two axes: modality (visual embeddings vs. embeddings of textual summaries) and temporal granularity (frame-level vs. clip-level). Existing retrieval strategies for <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> apply a single configuration, or a fixed fusion of the four, uniformly to every candidate chunk given a query—a <em id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">query-level</em> decision. However, this is suboptimal because the best configuration is a property of the chunk’s content, not of the query; a visually salient moment is most discriminable through visual embeddings, while a semantically rich moment is better surfaced through its textual summary, and granularity follows the same content dependence. We therefore propose <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> (<span id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">C</span>hunk-<span id="S1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">A</span>ware <span id="S1.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">R</span>eranking for <span id="S1.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">V</span>ideo <span id="S1.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">E</span>vidence) in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, a novel framework that exploits multi-modal cross-encoder signals to assign each candidate chunk its winning configuration based on its query relevance. The key idea is to re-purpose reranking, which is a tool widely used in text-RAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> but largely unexplored in <span id="S1.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>, as a mechanism for <em id="S1.p5.1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chunk-level</em> configuration decision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> operates in two stages. First, parallel retrieval runs four parallel retrievers, one per configuration, each contributing its top-<math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> chunks to a shared candidate pool. Second, a multi-modal cross-encoder re-scores each candidate under its retrieving configuration (<em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, by query–text relevance for textual retrievals and by query-visual relevance for visual retrievals). Sorting these configuration-specific scores yields the final ranking, in which chunks from different configurations are interleaved and each chunk carries its retrieving configuration onward. The final top-<math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> chunks then serve both stages of the <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> pipeline. For retrieval, they are the output of <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span>, where the chunk-level decision lies in which configuration scored each chunk highest. For generation, the same chunks are augmented to the query, but each is supplied to the generator under its retrieving configuration, so the representation fed to the generator varies per chunk. Importantly, our experiments reveal that this chunk-level selection is not only beneficial at the retrieval stage, but becomes even more impactful when carried into generation, leading to further gains in answer accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments on <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span> reveal four key findings: (1) <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> significantly outperforms eight baseline methods on both retrieval and generation, a comparison enabled by <span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span>’s stage-wise measurability; (2) a modality–granularity ablation reveals large performance gaps across configurations at both stages, with no single configuration uniformly optimal, motivating chunk-level rather than query-level decisions; (3) <span id="S1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span>’s chunk-level decisions distribute fairly evenly across the four configurations, showing that per-chunk diversity is genuine rather than collapsing onto a single dominant choice; and (4) at generation, <span id="S1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> even surpasses a trained query-level router that learns to pick one configuration per query, without any additional training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval-Augmented Generation in Text.</span> RAG improves generation by retrieving query-relevant evidence from an external text corpus and conditioning the generator on it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. Progress has centered on retrieval, since retrieval quality largely dictates final-answer quality. One axis advances the retriever, from sparse and dense retrievers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite> to hybrid sparse–dense pipelines that combine complementary signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. A second reformulates the query through rewriting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite> or expansion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite> to make retrieval intent explicit. A third addresses the limited precision of first-stage retrievers through reranking, where a cross-encoder re-scores candidates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>. Together, these advances substantially improve retrieval quality, with gains that largely translate into downstream generation accuracy. They are, however, developed for text and do not readily generalize to retrieving evidence from video.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval-Augmented Generation in Long Video.</span> Long-form video understanding has traditionally relied on universal video representations such as sparse frame sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>, visual-token compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite>, or full-video textualization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>. Yet, these encode the entire video into a query-independent view, diluting query-relevant evidence as length grows. This motivates <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>, which mirrors text RAG by retrieving query-relevant evidence before generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The main challenge in <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> is that retrieval and generation become substantially more complex than in text. A video chunk is continuous in time and inherently multimodal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite>, expressible through visual frames or derived textual abstractions, and at temporal granularities from individual frames to segments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. Existing work explores these axes from complementary angles. On the modality side, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al. [28]</a></cite> retrieves chunks using multimodal embeddings, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al. [42]</a></cite> augments retrieval with visually-aligned auxiliary text such as OCR and ASR. On the granularity side, hierarchical and tree-structured representations index the video at multiple temporal scales <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>. A separate line replaces one-shot retrieval with iterative, agentic probing, where an LLM repeatedly fetches frames or segments to refine its evidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, these methods commit to a single modality–granularity choice (or a fixed fusion of all of them) per query, and analysis is weighted toward downstream QA accuracy, leaving retrieval quality itself less directly examined. As we discuss next, this stems from the benchmark side, where existing datasets are not designed to evaluate retrieval separately from generation. Our work addresses both gaps by treating modality and granularity as explicit design axes selected at the chunk-level, and by introducing a benchmark that makes retrieval and generation independently measurable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Video Benchmark Datasets for QA.</span> Video QA benchmarks have progressed from short third-person clips <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite> toward longer videos and, increasingly, egocentric settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>. This shift mirrors the natural target of <span id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>: personal video has emerged as a primary retrieval domain, as wearable devices accumulate hour-scale first-person video and personal agents increasingly operate over it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>]</cite>. However, existing benchmarks remain limited in this regime. For example, EgoSchema (3-minute clips) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> QaEgo4D (short Ego4D intervals) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, Video-MME (<math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>60 min) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, and LongVideoBench (<math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>1 hour) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">While Ego4D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and EgoLife <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> provide hour-scale source video, their accompanying queries fall short on two counts. First, public queries are scarce and serve different evaluation goals. Ego4D’s queries are scarce and belong to the Episodic Memory benchmark, which evaluates retrieval alone, while EgoLifeQA targets long-term memory aggregation across hours and days. Second, neither targets <span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> evaluation, and thus lacks the three properties it requires: (i) non-recurring evidence, (ii) visual grounding, and (iii) evidence localization. These ensure that retrieval and generation can be assessed independently and jointly, with correct answers tied to the labeled evidence rather than to spurious cues. CG-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and MM-Lifelong <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> make partial progress by checking shortcut answerability and clue grounding, addressing (ii) but leaving (i) and (iii) untouched. Our benchmark closes this gap by jointly enforcing all three properties on hour-scale egocentric video.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Formulating VideoRAG along Two Design Axes</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> as a retrieve-then-generate process over long videos. While prior studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite> establish this pipeline, two design choices remain underspecified. First, the same chunk admits multiple representations across modality (visual vs. textual) and granularity (frame vs. clip), but these are typically combined at fixed scales rather than treated as decision variables. Second, the relationship between the representation used for retrieval and the one used for generation has received little attention, with the two typically tied to a single choice by default.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following standard text RAG practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, a <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> system partitions a long video <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> into non-overlapping, fixed-interval temporal chunks that serve as retrieval units, yielding <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{|}V|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></msub><mi>V</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{v_{1},\dots,v_{|}V|\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We default to 2-minute chunks, matching the empirical 2–3 minute span of bounded semantic content in long-form video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. Each such chunk, however, is fundamentally richer than a text passage: <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> admits multiple representations <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m,g}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m,g}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> along two complementary axes: a modality <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{\mathrm{vis},\mathrm{text}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>vis</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>text</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{\mathrm{vis},\mathrm{text}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (visual features vs. textual abstractions) and a temporal granularity <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\{\mathrm{frame},\mathrm{clip}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>frame</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>clip</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\{\mathrm{frame},\mathrm{clip}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (frame-level details vs. clip-level summaries).<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          We instantiate <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with two values each, giving four <math id="footnote2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> configurations for ease of exposition. Our formulation readily accommodates additional modalities and granularities.</span></span></span> Given a query <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, a retriever <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> matches <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> against chunk representations to select <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> candidate chunks <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{q}\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{q}\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math>, and an MLLM-based generator <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> produces an answer from those chunks under a chosen representation:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{a}=\mathcal{G}\!\left(q,\;\{\phi_{m_{g},g_{g}}(v)\}_{v\in V_{q}}\right)~~{\rm where}~~V_{q}=\mathcal{R}\!\left(q,\;\{\phi_{m_{r},g_{r}}(v)\}_{v\in V}\right),\qquad" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.484em;" width="0.484em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>where</mi><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.678em;" width="0.678em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{a}=\mathcal{G}\!\left(q,\;\{\phi_{m_{g},g_{g}}(v)\}_{v\in V_{q}}\right)~~{\rm where}~~V_{q}=\mathcal{R}\!\left(q,\;\{\phi_{m_{r},g_{r}}(v)\}_{v\in V}\right),\qquad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{r},g_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{r},g_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{g},g_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{g},g_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> are the configurations chosen for retrieval and generation, respectively; and <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m,g}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m,g}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is the representation of chunk <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> under modality <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and granularity <math id="S3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. The goal is twofold: <math id="S3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> should contain the chunk that supports the answer, and <math id="S3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> should match the reference answer <math id="S3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, whereas a text chunk has a single canonical form, a video chunk forces <span id="S3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> to decide <em id="S3.p2.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which <math id="S3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> to use for retrieval and which for generation</em>, and these decisions need not coincide.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now describe how the two design axes are instantiated in this work. Full implementation details (<em id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, decoding hyperparameters, temperature) are deferred to Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Implementation details ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, and the prompt templates used are provided in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Prompts ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chunk Modality.</span> The modality axis <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{\mathrm{vis},\mathrm{text}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>vis</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>text</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{\mathrm{vis},\mathrm{text}\}</annotation></semantics></math> exposes a chunk <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> through two complementary channels. The <em id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">visual</em> modality encodes <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> as raw visual features extracted by an MLLM-based multimodal encoder (Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> by default), preserving fine-grained appearance and motion cues that resist verbalization. In contrast, the <em id="S3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">textual</em> modality describes <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in natural language by prompting an MLLM (Qwen3-VL-8B) to summarize the chunk <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and embeds the resulting text with the same encoder, surfacing semantic structure (<em id="S3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, what is happening, where, and with whom) at the cost of compressing pixel-level detail. Neither modality is universally preferable; the more useful one depends on the query and chunk content, as textual abstractions surface semantics invisible to raw pixels, while visual features capture appearance and motion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chunk Granularity.</span> Even within a fixed modality, the temporal scope at which a chunk is read can vary. The granularity axis <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\{\mathrm{frame},\mathrm{clip}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>frame</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>clip</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\{\mathrm{frame},\mathrm{clip}\}</annotation></semantics></math> exposes a chunk <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> at two contrasting scales. The <em id="S3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">frame</em>-level retains fine-grained, per-frame information through a small set of keyframes. Specifically, we cluster all frame embeddings in the chunk with <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-means++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> (<math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math> by default) and take the cluster centroids as keyframes, covering distinct visual states rather than uniform time samples. On the other hand, the <em id="S3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">clip</em>-level instead treats <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> as a single unit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">They are then realized in a modality-specific way. Under the visual modality, the frame-level mean-pools the keyframe embeddings while the clip-level encodes the entire chunk into one embedding. Under the textual modality, the same MLLM produces a summary capped at <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, applied per keyframe (concatenated before embedding) for the frame-level and over the entire chunk for the clip-level. This cap acts as an upper bound that accommodates detailed clip descriptions used in prior multimodal summarization work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> while preventing verbose outputs from dominating retrieval and generation context. Neither granularity is universally preferable; frame-level forms capture transient details but lose the surrounding flow, while clip-level forms retain temporal context but blur transient details, so the more useful scale depends on the query and the chunk content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scope and Objective.</span> With <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> now made explicit, two questions follow: <em id="S3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> it should be decided and <em id="S3.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">at what level</em>. In other words, for each chunk <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, which representation <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m,g}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m,g}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> should be passed to the retriever and the generator? The simplest choice is to fix one <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> per query and apply it uniformly across chunks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>; agentic variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite> issue multiple retrievals per query, but each call still commits to a single <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math>. This leaves room for finer-grained control, since the most useful <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> may differ across chunks even within a single query. For instance, identifying a specific object visible in only one keyframe is best handled by a frame-level visual embedding, while describing how an activity unfolds over the chunk is better expressed by a clip-level textual summary. We therefore explore deciding <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.p7.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">per chunk</em>, carrying the choice consistently into both retrieval and generation while keeping the standard retrieve-then-generate pipeline intact.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Construction of V-RAGBench</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diagnosing how the two design axes interact at retrieval and generation requires a faithful benchmark in which the two stages are measurable both in isolation and in their interaction, which in turn requires every query to be tied to a uniquely sufficient evidence chunk. We therefore construct <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">V-RAGBench</span>, a benchmark of <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle</annotation></semantics></math>query, evidence chunk, answer<math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> triplets built from hour-scale egocentric videos through the four stages (see Figure <a href="#A3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Appendix C Dataset ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)—namely source selection, event extraction and deduplication, query generation, and filtering—described below. Our construction process is further verified by human study (see Section <a href="#A3.SS5" title="C.5 QA Filter Details ‣ Appendix C Dataset ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.5</span></a>): on 577 sampled QA pairs, annotators show substantial inter-annotator agreement (Cohen’s <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0.63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.63</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0.63</annotation></semantics></math>), jointly flagging only 3.1% as unanswerable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Source Video Selection.</span> We focus on egocentric video as the source domain for <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>. Unlike third-person long videos (<em id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, movies, lectures) organized around a single narrative arc, first-person streams capture continuous, weakly structured daily activity in which the same actor revisits similar scenes and routines, making retrieval over personal memory both more realistic and substantially harder. Thus, we draw source videos from two complementary egocentric corpora, Ego4D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and EgoLife <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>, retaining only videos <em id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">longer than one hour</em> and applying stratified sampling over length bins so that the corpus spans a wide range of durations. This yields a search corpus of <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="216" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>216</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">216</annotation></semantics></math> personal videos in total, comprising <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math> EgoLife recordings (avg. <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="379.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>379.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">379.1</annotation></semantics></math> min., <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="177.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>177.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">177.8</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="552.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>552.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">552.7</annotation></semantics></math> min.) and <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="174" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>174</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">174</annotation></semantics></math> Ego4D videos (avg. <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>86.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86.0</annotation></semantics></math> min., <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60.0</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="423.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>423.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">423.6</annotation></semantics></math> min.); see Table <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ C.1 Dataset statistics ‣ Appendix C Dataset ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Reflecting the personal nature of <span id="S4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>, retrieval is performed within each wearer’s own corpus, so every query is grounded in their video history. Per-domain and per-length statistics are in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Dataset statistics ‣ Appendix C Dataset ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Event Extraction and Deduplication.</span> This stage converts each long video into a small set of distinct event chunks that form a non-redundant evidence pool for QA generation. For each video, we encode every sampled frame with EVA02-E-14-plus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> (a CLIP-based vision encoder) and apply kernel temporal segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> on the resulting frame embeddings to cut the video into semantically coherent, time-contiguous segments. The number of segments is capped at <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>150</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">150</annotation></semantics></math> for the shorter Ego4D videos and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="900" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>900</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">900</annotation></semantics></math> for the day-long EgoLife recordings, with each segment constrained to <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math>s. We then re-embed each segment as a whole with Qwen3-VL-Embedding-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> in video mode and cluster the embeddings with k-Means++ (<math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\!=\!50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\!=\!50</annotation></semantics></math> for Ego4D, <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\!=\!300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\!=\!300</annotation></semantics></math> for EgoLife), retaining only the segment closest to each centroid as its representative. This suppresses recurring routines (<em id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, repeated meal preparation), leaving chunks that correspond to <em id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distinct events</em>—a prerequisite for QA generation, since queries grounded in recurring events would admit multiple sufficient evidence chunks; while natural in the real world, such queries are excluded here for rigorous <span id="S4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> evaluation. Details of deduplication and statistics of this stage are in appendix <a href="#A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" title="Event Chunk Construction Details. ‣ C.4 Event selection and de-duplication details ‣ Appendix C Dataset ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Query Generation.</span> For each event chunk, we prompt Gemini-3-flash-preview to generate up to three candidate queries spanning three predefined categories (See Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Definitions of the query categories ‣ Appendix C Dataset ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>), with the model itself deciding which are applicable rather than forcing all three. The prompt further enforces <em id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contextual localization</em>, requiring every query to embed anchor information from its source event so that the query alone describes the situation enough to be matched against the right chunk; this lets <span id="S4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span> dispense with direct temporal cues such as timestamps or when phrases, forcing retrieval to rely purely on content (full prompt and examples in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Prompts ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>). Each generation produces a triple <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle q,\,[t_{s},t_{e}],\,a\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle q,\,[t_{s},t_{e}],\,a\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> of query, evidence time frames, and <em id="S4.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">open-ended</em> form rather than a multiple-choice option, ensuring that downstream evaluation reflects the generator’s true ability rather than being inflated by random guessing over a fixed choice set. This yields 67,370 candidate <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\text{query},\,\text{evidence chunk},\,\text{answer}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mtext>query</mtext><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mtext>evidence chunk</mtext><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mtext>answer</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\text{query},\,\text{evidence chunk},\,\text{answer}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> triples for the filtering pipeline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Post-hoc Filtering.</span> While event deduplication enforces content uniqueness at the event level, the generated queries are natural-language artifacts whose grounding and uniqueness must be verified at the query level. Specifically, the two properties <em id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">visual grounding</em>—the answer is not implied by the query or recoverable from its priors—and <em id="S4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evidence localization</em>—the answer is not recoverable from non-evidence chunks—are not yet guaranteed and must be enforced post-hoc.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We thus apply five filters targeting these properties along with query-level redundancy. (1) <span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Semantic Similarity</span>: queries too close in Qwen3-VL-8B embedding space are removed, with per-video caps of <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> (Ego4D) and <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math> (EgoLife) to prevent any single video from dominating; (2) <span id="S4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Answerability</span>: GPT-5.2-chat is asked whether each query is answerable from its source clip, and unanswerable ones are removed; (3) <span id="S4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Shortcut Bias</span>: queries GPT-5.2-chat answers correctly without visual context are discarded; (4) <span id="S4.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Empirical Answerability</span>: Gemini-2.5-flash must produce a correct answer from the source clip, judged by an LLM-as-a-judge; and (5) <span id="S4.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evidence Uniqueness</span>: for each query, we retrieve the top-<math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> chunks while <em id="S4.p6.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">excluding</em> the true evidence, and discard the query if Gemini-2.5-flash can still answer correctly from this non-evidence set, indicating the answer is recoverable elsewhere.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The surviving queries are (1) lexically and semantically distinct, (2, 4) grounded in sufficient evidence, (3) free of parametric and commonsense shortcuts, and (5) not recoverable from any other chunk of the same video. Since the three query categories are unevenly populated after filtering, we randomly subsample each category to match the smallest one (<math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\!=\!700" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>700</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\!=\!700</annotation></semantics></math> per category), yielding a balanced dataset of <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{,}100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{,}100</annotation></semantics></math> queries in total (<math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}800" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,800</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}800</annotation></semantics></math> train, <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math> test).<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          The split train set is specifically used for the analysis in-depth study in Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 6.2 In-depth Analysis of Interleaved Chunk Representations ‣ 6 Evaluation on V-RAGBench ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, where we train two retrieval routers.</span></span></span> We defer per-stage details to Appendix <a href="#A3.SS5" title="C.5 QA Filter Details ‣ Appendix C Dataset ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>CARVE: Chunk-Aware Reranking for Video Evidence</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now introduce <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span>, a novel <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> method that decides the modality–granularity configuration <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> at the <em id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chunk</em>-level rather than at the query-level, and propagates that decision consistently from retrieval into generation. Concretely, <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> operates in two simple stages, namely parallel candidate pooling and chunk-adaptive reranking, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1: Parallel Candidate Pooling.</span> Given a query <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, this stage builds a <em id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">candidate pool</em> by performing four parallel retrievals, one per configuration <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{\mathrm{vis},\mathrm{text}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>vis</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>text</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{\mathrm{vis},\mathrm{text}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\{\mathrm{frame},\mathrm{clip}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>frame</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>clip</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\{\mathrm{frame},\mathrm{clip}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The four embeddings <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m,g}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m,g}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> of a video chunk <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 Formulating VideoRAG along Two Design Axes ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), one per configuration, are precomputed offline for every chunk in the corpus, so each retrieval at inference time only requires encoding the query <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> into an embedding and performing a fast nearest-neighbor lookup against the precomputed chunk embeddings. That is, no additional chunk-encoding cost is incurred in this stage, regardless of how many configurations are queried in parallel.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each configuration <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the dot-product similarity <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\,\cdot\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\,\cdot\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> between the query embedding <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and each chunk embedding <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m,g}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m,g}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, and keep the <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> chunks with the highest scores as the top-<math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> retrieval list <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{(m,g)}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{(m,g)}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> for that configuration:</p>
<table id="S5.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{(m,g)}(q)\;=\;\text{top-}k\text{ chunks }v\text{ ranked by }\langle q,\,\phi_{m,g}(v)\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mtext>top-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> chunks </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> ranked by </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{(m,g)}(q)\;=\;\text{top-}k\text{ chunks }v\text{ ranked by }\langle q,\,\phi_{m,g}(v)\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The four lists are then merged into a configuration-tagged candidate pool <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P}(q)</annotation></semantics></math>, where each retrieved chunk is paired with the configuration <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> that retrieved the chunk <math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P}(q)\;=\;\bigcup_{(m,g)}\Big\{\,\big(\,\underbrace{v}_{\text{chunk}},\;\underbrace{(m,g)}_{\text{tag}}\,\big)\;:\;v\in C_{(m,g)}(q)\,\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>chunk</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>tag</mtext></munder><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.558em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P}(q)\;=\;\bigcup_{(m,g)}\Big\{\,\big(\,\underbrace{v}_{\text{chunk}},\;\underbrace{(m,g)}_{\text{tag}}\,\big)\;:\;v\in C_{(m,g)}(q)\,\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The same chunk <math id="S5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> may appear under multiple tags if it was retrieved by more than one configuration. These tags are preserved into Stage 2 rather than collapsed away, since they are precisely the per-configuration signal that lets the next stage decide a winning <math id="S5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> for each chunk.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2: Chunk-Adaptive Reranking.</span> Given the candidate pool <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> from Stage 1, this stage selects the final top-<math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> chunks, while assigning each surviving chunk a single <em id="S5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">winning</em> configuration <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m^{*},g^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m^{*},g^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the chunk embeddings in <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> live in different modalities depending on the tag—textual when <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\mathrm{text}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>text</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\mathrm{text}</annotation></semantics></math>, visual when <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\mathrm{vis}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>vis</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\mathrm{vis}</annotation></semantics></math>—we use a multi-modal cross-encoder <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CE}(\cdot,\,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CE}(\cdot,\,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
          Temporal granularity <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> does not change the modality of <math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m,g}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m,g}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, since frame-level and clip-level forms under the same modality share the same encoder branch and embedding space, differing only in temporal scope (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Formulating VideoRAG along Two Design Axes ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</span></span></span> that jointly attends to a textual query and either textual or visual chunk modality. We use Qwen3-VL-Reranker-2B as the default reranker, though performance remains consistent across rerankers, as discussed in Section <a href="#A6.SS5" title="F.5 Reranker Backbone Robustness ‣ Appendix F Additional Analysis ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, each chunk <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> is re-scored <em id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only</em> under the tag <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,g)</annotation></semantics></math> that retrieved it, not under all four configurations. The rerank score of a tagged candidate <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,(m,g))\in\mathcal{P}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,(m,g))\in\mathcal{P}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> is computed:</p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{s}\big(q;\,(v,(m,g))\big)\;=\;\mathrm{CE}\big(q,\,\phi_{m,g}(v)\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>CE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{s}\big(q;\,(v,(m,g))\big)\;=\;\mathrm{CE}\big(q,\,\phi_{m,g}(v)\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">For each chunk <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, we keep only its highest-scoring tag as its winning configuration:</p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(m^{*}_{v},\,g^{*}_{v})\;=\;\argmax_{(m,g)\,:\,(v,(m,g))\in\mathcal{P}(q)}\tilde{s}\big(q;\,(v,(m,g))\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.448em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m^{*}_{v},\,g^{*}_{v})\;=\;\argmax_{(m,g)\,:\,(v,(m,g))\in\mathcal{P}(q)}\tilde{s}\big(q;\,(v,(m,g))\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CE</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CE}</annotation></semantics></math> scores are comparable across modalities, so chunks can be ranked together by their winning scores, yielding the final top-<math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> list (<math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{*}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{*}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>) with each chunk tagged by its winning configuration:</p>
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{*}_{q}\;=\;\Big\{\,\big(\,v,\,(m^{*}_{v},g^{*}_{v})\,\big)\;:\;v\in\text{top-}k\text{ chunks ranked by }\tilde{s}\big(q;\,(v,(m^{*}_{v},g^{*}_{v}))\big)\,\Big\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.558em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>top-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> chunks ranked by </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{*}_{q}\;=\;\Big\{\,\big(\,v,\,(m^{*}_{v},g^{*}_{v})\,\big)\;:\;v\in\text{top-}k\text{ chunks ranked by }\tilde{s}\big(q;\,(v,(m^{*}_{v},g^{*}_{v}))\big)\,\Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.4" class="ltx_p">This chunk-adaptive reranking improves retrieval by judging each chunk under its most discriminative configuration and letting chunks from different configurations compete on a comparable scale, so the top-<math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> naturally interleaves the strongest evidence across modalities and granularities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modality-Interleaved Generation.</span> <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{*}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{*}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> benefits not only retrieval but also generation. Unlike prior work that fixes a single <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{g},g_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{g},g_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> for the whole query <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> or fuses several representations uniformly, we expose each chunk to the generator <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S5.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only</em> through its winning representation <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m^{*}_{v},g^{*}_{v}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m^{*}_{v},g^{*}_{v}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{a}\;=\;\mathcal{G}\!\left(q,\;\big\{\phi_{m^{*}_{v},\,g^{*}_{v}}(v)\;:\;(v,(m^{*}_{v},g^{*}_{v}))\in V^{*}_{q}\big\}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.484em;" width="0.484em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{a}\;=\;\mathcal{G}\!\left(q,\;\big\{\phi_{m^{*}_{v},\,g^{*}_{v}}(v)\;:\;(v,(m^{*}_{v},g^{*}_{v}))\in V^{*}_{q}\big\}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.2" class="ltx_p">The generator context thus mixes representations chunk by chunk, with each chunk rendered under its winning configuration. Empirically, this propagation from retrieval to generation—not the retrieval gain alone—is where <span id="S5.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span>’s strongest improvements appear.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Evaluation on V-RAGBench</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation of <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> on the test set of <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span> consists of three parts. First, we compare <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> against recent <span id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> methods on both retrieval and generation to demonstrate its superiority (Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6.1 Main Result: Performance on Retrieval and Generation ‣ 6 Evaluation on V-RAGBench ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Second, we study how modality and temporal granularity should be selected under our method, showing that <span id="S6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span>’s chunk-level choices outperform query-level alternatives (Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.1 Main Result: Performance on Retrieval and Generation ‣ 6 Evaluation on V-RAGBench ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Third, we further show the efficiency and effectiveness of chunk-level decisions via latency, selection distribution, and comparison against two trained query-level routers (Tables <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.2 In-depth Analysis of Interleaved Chunk Representations ‣ 6 Evaluation on V-RAGBench ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>–<a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 6.2 In-depth Analysis of Interleaved Chunk Representations ‣ 6 Evaluation on V-RAGBench ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compared Methods.</span> We compare <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> against eight RAG methods applicable to <span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>, which differ in modality, granularity, and fusion strategy. We group them into two categories. (i) <span id="S6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System-level</span> methods apply a fixed policy regardless of the query, including VideoRAG-A <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, which linearly interpolates text and visual modality scores; GQR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, which refines query embeddings at test time via complementary retriever signals; and the multimodal encoders, namely Freeret <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite> and GME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>, which retrieve in a single embedding space. (ii) <span id="S6.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Query-level</span> methods adapt the policy per query but apply the same decision to all chunks, including RRF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, which expands a query into variants and fuses retrieval ranks; DAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, which dynamically weights dense and sparse retrievers via LLM-assessed reference scores; and VideoRAG-B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> and UniversalRAG(-LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>, which respectively decompose or route a query across auxiliary databases spanning different modalities and granularities, where (-LoRA) denotes its LoRA-trained variant on the training set of <span id="S6.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span>. For fairness, all baselines share the same chunk embeddings as <span id="S6.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> and differ only in how representations are selected or combined. See Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Baseline Implementations ‣ Appendix D Implementation details ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> for implementation details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span> We evaluate retrieval and generation separately. For retrieval, we report two metrics: <em id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nDCG@<math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></em>, which rewards methods that rank the annotated evidence higher; and <em id="S6.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Recall@<math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></em>, which measures whether the annotated evidence appears among the top-<math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> retrieved chunks. Since retrieval relies on deterministic embedding similarity, these metrics carry no run-to-run variance.
For generation, we report the aggregate <em id="S6.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pass rate</em>, defined as the fraction of queries whose generated answer is judged correct against the reference by an LLM-as-a-judge (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B), which is necessary since answers are in open-ended form rather than multiple choice (see the prompt in Figure <a href="#A7.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ G.3 LLM Judge Prompt ‣ Appendix G Prompts ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). To assess generalization across generators, we evaluate each retrieval method with three backbones: {Qwen3-VL-8B, Qwen3-VL-32B, and Gemma-4-26B}. We fix <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math> throughout experiments, except in Appendix <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Effect of Top-K ‣ Appendix F Additional Analysis ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a> where we vary <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to show that <span id="S6.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> consistently outperforms baselines. Detailed definitions of all metrics are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 Evaluation Metrics ‣ Appendix D Implementation details ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Main Result: Performance on Retrieval and Generation</h3>

