</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reranking Configuration Strategy.</span>
We next examine whether <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span>’s reranking rule of rescoring each candidate under its retrieving configuration in Eqs. (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In 5 CARVE: Chunk-Aware Reranking for Video Evidence ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)–(<a href="#S5.E5" title="In 5 CARVE: Chunk-Aware Reranking for Video Evidence ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) is the right strategy. Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.2 In-depth Analysis of Interleaved Chunk Representations ‣ 6 Evaluation on V-RAGBench ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> compares against three alternatives: (i) applying a single fixed configuration to all candidates regardless of how they were retrieved, (ii) assigning a configuration randomly to each candidate (Random), and (iii) feeding all four configurations jointly into the reranker without selection (Concatenation). <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> substantially outperforms all alternatives. This shows that scoring each chunk under its retrieving configuration tag, rather than under a fixed or heuristic alternative, is what drives the gain. In particular, Concatenation fails to match <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> despite using all four configurations jointly, indicating that the chunk-level alignment between retrieval and reranking matters more than access to more representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> also achieves a favorable accuracy–latency trade-off. Reranking visual features is costly due to the larger token count (<math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.1</annotation></semantics></math>s and <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.0</annotation></semantics></math>s for visual-only configurations), but by passing only each chunk’s winning configuration, <span id="S6.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> runs at <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.4</annotation></semantics></math>s while still achieving the best retrieval, substantially faster than visual-only and Concatenation (<math id="S6.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.2</annotation></semantics></math>s). The same trend for retrieval performance and latency holds under a different reranker backbone (LamRA-Rank-7B), as seen in Appendix <a href="#A6.SS5" title="F.5 Reranker Backbone Robustness ‣ Appendix F Additional Analysis ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T5" class="ltx_table" style="width:39%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Comparison of <span id="S6.T5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> against query-level routing in generation.</figcaption>
<table id="S6.T5.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T5.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T5.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">Method</th>
<td id="S6.T5.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">Pass Rate</td>
<td id="S6.T5.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">Latency</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T5.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T5.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">m={text}, g={frame}</th>
<td id="S6.T5.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">0.207</td>
<td id="S6.T5.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">3.0s</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T5.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T5.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">m={text}, g={clip}</th>
<td id="S6.T5.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">0.158</td>
<td id="S6.T5.4.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">1.7s</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T5.4.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T5.4.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">m={vis}, g={frame}</th>
<td id="S6.T5.4.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">0.323</td>
<td id="S6.T5.4.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">4.4s</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T5.4.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T5.4.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">m={vis}, g={clip}</th>
<td id="S6.T5.4.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">0.293</td>
<td id="S6.T5.4.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">5.9s</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T5.4.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T5.4.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">Non-LLM Router w. Training</th>
<td id="S6.T5.4.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">0.329</td>
<td id="S6.T5.4.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">5.7s</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T5.4.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T5.4.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">LLM Router w. Training</th>
<td id="S6.T5.4.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">0.310</td>
<td id="S6.T5.4.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">5.3s</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T5.4.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T5.4.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;"><span id="S6.T5.4.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> (Ours)</th>
<td id="S6.T5.4.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;"><span id="S6.T5.4.8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.357</span></td>
<td id="S6.T5.4.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.25pt 6.5pt;">4.6s</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison against Query-Level Routing.</span>
We finally examine whether <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span>’s chunk-level interleaving of representations fed to the generator is more effective than rendering all retrieved chunks under a single, query-level representation. Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 6.2 In-depth Analysis of Interleaved Chunk Representations ‣ 6 Evaluation on V-RAGBench ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compares <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> with two baselines: (i) single-fixed routing, which always renders chunks under the same configuration regardless of the query, and (ii) trained query-level routers, which select one configuration per query and apply it uniformly to all chunks for generation. The latter includes a non-LLM router (an MLP head over a frozen BGE-large-en-v1.5 encoder) and an LLM router (Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct fine-tuned with LoRA), both trained on the <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span> training split (see training details in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Baseline Implementations ‣ Appendix D Implementation details ‣ Rethinking RAG in Long Videos: What to Retrieve and How to Use It?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>). <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> outperforms all baselines despite using no training, while the routers are fine-tuned on the <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span> training split. This shows that no single configuration suits every chunk in a query, a limitation <span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> sidesteps by varying the representation chunk by chunk through the same reranking step, at competitive generation latency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we address two emerging gaps in <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span>. On the benchmark side, we introduce <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span>, a collection of <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle</annotation></semantics></math>query, evidence chunk, answer<math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> triplets over hour-scale egocentric videos that jointly enforce non-recurring evidence, visual grounding, and evidence localization for decoupled evaluation of retrieval and generation. On the methodological side, <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> reframes modality and temporal granularity as a chunk-level decision via parallel candidate pooling and chunk-adaptive reranking, propagating each chunk’s winning configuration into generation. On <span id="S7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V-RAGBench</span>, <span id="S7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> outperforms eight recent baselines for <span id="S7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> on both stages; and on generation side, it even surpasses the trained query-level routers without additional training, with winning configurations distributing evenly across all configurations rather than collapsing onto a dominant one.</p>
</div>
</section>
