</figure>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.4 </span>Effect of Top-K</h3>

<div id="A6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also vary the final retrieval depth <math id="A6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to test whether <span id="A6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> is sensitive to the number of chunks passed to the generator. This setting is important because increasing <math id="A6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> can improve recall by adding more candidate evidence, but it can also introduce distractor chunks that make generation harder. A robust <span id="A6.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VideoRAG</span> method should therefore maintain strong performance across different retrieval depths, rather than relying on a single tuned value of <math id="A6.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across the tested values of <math id="A6.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="A6.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> remains stable and consistently competitive, showing that its gains do not depend on a particular retrieval budget. This indicates that chunk-wise parallel reranking improves the ordering of evidence itself, rather than merely benefiting from retrieving more chunks. In other words, <span id="A6.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> is effective because it selects better-ranked evidence, not because it relies on a larger context window. This experiment shows that <span id="A6.SS4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">chunk-adaptive reranking produces a robust evidence ranking across retrieval depths, making <span id="A6.SS4.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CARVE</span> less sensitive to the final top-<math id="A6.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> choice</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13141v1/k_ablation_heatmap.png" id="A6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/180;" width="476" height="180" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Heatmaps of Recall@5 and nDCG@5 under different final retrieval depths.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
